  "result": "Fixed. The command now prints `27.06`.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the digits after the decimal point on the left (`rjust`), so `\"12.5\"` was read as 12.05, giving 1205 cents instead of 1250. That made the quote 26.09 instead of 27.06. I changed it to pad on the right (`ljust`), so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Prices with two decimals or none (`\"12.34\"`, `\"12\"`) were already handled correctly and still are.\n\nThe check: 1250 \u00d7 2 = 2500, plus 8.25% tax (206.25, rounded to 206) = 2706, which prints as 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. All 6 pass. None of them test `to_cents` directly, which is why this slipped through, so a test for one-decimal input would be worth adding.\n\nThere's a separate bug in the same function that I left alone: negative amounts with decimals come out wrong. For example, `\"-1.50\"` gives -50 cents instead of -150, because the decimal part is added to the negative whole part instead of subtracted.",
